Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; painted storybook look, soft watercolor and ink, warm nursery colors.

FADE IN:

EXT. NURSERY WINDOW - CHRISTMAS MORNING

Snow dusts the sill. Inside the glass, a child's stocking bulges on the bedpost.

(SOUND: distant church bells, soft crackle of a hearth)

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT sits wedged in the top of THE BOY's stocking, spotted brown and white velveteen, real thread whiskers, ears lined with pink sateen, a sprig of holly between his paws.

(SOUND: tissue paper rustling, children's excited voices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

Nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and a clockwork mouse tumble from the stocking. THE BOY, about seven, fair hair, nightshirt, hugs the Rabbit for a long, happy moment.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and Uncles fill the doorway with parcels. In the rush of new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit is set aside on the nursery floor.

(SOUND: laughter, paper tearing, footsteps receding)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

The toy cupboard stands open. Mechanical toys gleam on the shelf. The MODEL BOAT, paint worn, sits stiffly. TIMOTHY, a jointed wooden lion, holds his head high. The Velveteen Rabbit lies alone near the fender, shy and still.

(SOUND: clock ticking, soft nursery hush)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a long time he lived in the toy cupboard or on the nursery floor, and no one thought very much about him.

The mechanical toys turn their painted faces away. The Rabbit's thread whiskers droop.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Being only made of velveteen, some of the more expensive toys quite snubbed him.

Beside him lies the SKIN HORSE — very old, brown coat bald in patches showing the seams, most of the hairs in his tail pulled out. His glass eyes are gentle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The only person who was kind to him at all was the Skin Horse.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Firelight flickers on the fender. The Velveteen Rabbit and the Skin Horse lie side by side. Moonlight pools on the floorboards.

(SOUND: low fire crackle, wind at the window)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

The Skin Horse turns his worn head toward the Rabbit.

SKIN HORSE
When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

(SOUND: ember settles in the grate)

SKIN HORSE
Generally, by the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and your eyes drop out and you get loose in the joints and very shabby.

The Rabbit listens, still.

SKIN HORSE
But these things don't matter at all, because once you are Real you can't be ugly, except to people who don't understand.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

The Skin Horse only smiles, seams soft in the firelight.

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. That was a great many years ago; but once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

The Rabbit sighs. His pink sateen ears catch the glow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He longed to become Real, and yet the idea of growing shabby was rather sad.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

NANA, middle-aged, apron and practical dress, hair pinned tight, hurries about the room. THE BOY sits up in bed in his nightshirt, searching the covers.

(SOUND: floorboards creak, cupboard door bangs)

NANA
Where is that china dog?

She sees the toy cupboard open, swoops, and drags the Velveteen Rabbit out by one ear.

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She puts him into the Boy's arms. The Boy clasps him tight.

(SOUND: bedsprings, a contented sigh)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

The night-light burns on the mantelpiece. THE BOY hugs the Velveteen Rabbit under the bedclothes, rolling, pushing him under the pillow, then making a soft tunnel of sheets.

(SOUND: whispered breathing, distant supper dishes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night, and for many nights after, the Velveteen Rabbit slept in the Boy's bed.

THE BOY
(whispering)
See, Bunny — burrows. Like real rabbits.

The Rabbit snuggles under the Boy's warm chin. The Boy's hands clasp him all night long.

(MUSIC: gentle lullaby theme, soft and small)

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT (LATER)

Time has passed. The Rabbit's velveteen is shabbier. His tail is unsewn. The pink is rubbed from his nose. THE BOY sleeps with him still held close.

(SOUND: steady night breathing, clock chime far off)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier, and all the pink rubbed off his nose where the Boy had kissed him.

EXT. GARDEN - DAY

Spring light. THE BOY wheels a barrow over the grass. The Velveteen Rabbit rides inside. They picnic. The Boy builds a little hut of sticks under the raspberry canes.

(SOUND: birdsong, wheelbarrow squeak, leaves rustle)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Spring came, and they had long days in the garden, for wherever the Boy went the Rabbit went too.

EXT. GARDEN LAWN - DUSK

The Velveteen Rabbit lies alone on the wet lawn, earthy from the flower bed. Dew soaks his coat. NANA approaches with a candle, grumbling, and rubs him with a corner of her apron.

(SOUND: evening insects, Nana's footsteps on gravel)

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BED - NIGHT

THE BOY sits up in bed and stretches out his hands. Candlelight on his face. NANA holds the damp Rabbit.

(SOUND: bedclothes rustle, night-light hiss)

THE BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

The Velveteen Rabbit goes still in pure happiness. Into his boot-button eyes comes a look of wisdom and beauty.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the little Rabbit heard that he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last.

NANA
(next morning, lifting him)
I declare if that old Bunny hasn't got quite a knowing expression!

(MUSIC: warm resolving chord)

EXT. WOOD - JUNE EVENING

Tall bracken and tree-trunks. THE BOY sets the Velveteen Rabbit in a little nest among the ferns, then wanders off among the trees.

(SOUND: wood pigeons, soft wind in leaves, ants in grass)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the long June evenings the Boy liked to go there after tea to play.

The Rabbit watches ants run between his velvet paws. Two WILD RABBITS creep from the tall bracken — furry, brand-new, seams invisible, bodies changing shape as they move. Their noses twitch. They pad close.

(SOUND: soft pads on earth, bracken whispering)

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

WILD RABBIT
Ho! It's as easy as anything.

The Wild Rabbit gives a big hop sideways and stands on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
I don't believe you can!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I can! I can jump higher than anything!

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Velveteen Rabbit sits frozen. The back of him is all one piece, like a pincushion.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to!

The Wild Rabbit stretches his neck and looks.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

SECOND WILD RABBIT laughs. The first whirls and dances till the little Rabbit grows dizzy.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still!

The Wild Rabbit comes so close his long whiskers brush the Velveteen Rabbit's ear — then wrinkles his nose, flattens his ears, and jumps backwards.

(SOUND: sharp intake, sudden stillness)

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps. THE BOY runs past. With a stamp and a flash of white tails the two wild rabbits vanish into the bracken.

(SOUND: running feet, bracken thrashing, then quiet)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

Only ants run to and fro. The bracken sways. Little white moths flutter out as the sun sinks. The Boy returns and carries him home.

(MUSIC: thin lonely air)

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Weeks later. The Velveteen Rabbit is very old and shabby — whiskers loved off, ear linings grey, brown spots faded, shape nearly gone. THE BOY still holds him as beautiful.

(SOUND: rain on the window, soft cough)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He loved him so hard that he loved all his whiskers off. To him he was always beautiful, and that was all that the little Rabbit cared about.

THE BOY's face is flushed. He talks in his sleep. His little body burns so hot it warms the Rabbit through. Strange people come and go. A light burns all night. The Rabbit lies hidden under the bedclothes and never stirs.

(SOUND: feverish murmuring, footsteps, bottle clink)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away, and he knew that the Boy needed him.

INT. NURSERY - DAY

Bright sunny morning. Windows stand wide open. THE BOY has been carried out. The Velveteen Rabbit lies tangled in the bedclothes, only his head peeping out.

(SOUND: birds outside, low adult voices in the hall)

NANA and the DOCTOR, dark coat and bag, stand near the bed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The Boy was going to the seaside to-morrow. The room was to be disinfected, and all the books and toys that the Boy had played with in bed must be burnt.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

NANA hesitates over the shabby Rabbit.

DOCTOR
Nonsense! Get him a new one. He mustn't have that any more!

(SOUND: sack cloth snapped open)

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND FOWL-HOUSE - DUSK

A sack of old picture-books and rubbish sits in the corner behind the fowl-house. The gardener's tools lean nearby. The Velveteen Rabbit wriggles until his head comes through the untied opening. He shivers. His coat is thin and threadbare.

(SOUND: hens settling, evening wind, distant house door)

He looks toward the raspberry canes, tall and close like a jungle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought of those long sunlit hours in the garden — how happy they were — and a great sadness came over him.

(MUSIC: low aching string)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear trickles down his shabby velvet nose and falls to the ground.

(SOUND: single soft drop on earth)

Where the tear falls, a mysterious flower pushes up — slender emerald leaves, a blossom like a golden cup. The Rabbit forgets to cry and watches. The blossom opens. Out steps the NURSERY FAIRY — dress of pearl and dew-drops, flowers at her neck and in her hair, face like the most perfect flower.

(SOUND: petal hush, faint chime)

She gathers him up and kisses his damp velveteen nose.

NURSERY FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT looks up, almost remembering.

NURSERY FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

She holds him close.

NURSERY FAIRY
When they are old and worn out and the children don't need them any more, then I come and take them away with me and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

(MUSIC: rising wonder, light and clear)

EXT. WOOD - NIGHT

Moon risen. Bracken fronds shine like frosted silver. The NURSERY FAIRY flies with the little Rabbit into the open glade. Wild rabbits dance with their shadows on the velvet grass — then stop and stand in a ring, staring.

(SOUND: soft wing-rush, then hush of the glade)

NURSERY FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land, for he is going to live with you for ever and ever!

She kisses the little Rabbit again and sets him on the grass.

NURSERY FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

He sits quite still, shy, remembering his missing hind legs — and does not know the last kiss changed him altogether.

Something tickles his nose. Before he thinks, he lifts a hind toe to scratch it.

(SOUND: tiny scratch, then a gasp of joy)

He has hind legs. Brown fur, soft and shiny, instead of dingy velveteen. Ears twitch by themselves. Long whiskers brush the grass.

He gives one leap — then springs about the turf, jumping sideways, whirling as the others do.

(SOUND: soft thumps on turf, happy breath)

When at last he stops to look for the Fairy, she has gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

(MUSIC: bright open theme)

EXT. WOOD - SPRING DAY

Warm sun. THE BOY, a little taller, plays among the trees behind the house. Two rabbits creep from the bracken and peep at him. One is brown all over. The other has strange markings under his fur, as though long ago he had been spotted, and the spots still show through. About his soft nose and round black eyes there is something familiar.

(SOUND: spring birds, leaves underfoot, soft rabbit stir)

THE BOY goes still, watching.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The spotted rabbit holds his gaze a moment longer — then both slip back into the bracken.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

(MUSIC: gentle close, like a held breath then release)

(SOUND: wood settles into quiet birdsong)

FADE OUT.

THE END
